1. Purpose

Set up Kerr-Schild initial conditions.

The metric is the Kerr solution in Cartesian Kerr-Schild coordinates,

  g_ab = eta_ab + 2 H l_a l_b ,

with the spin along the z axis and the black hole at the coordinate origin.
Kerr-Schild coordinates are horizon penetrating, so the grid contains the
region inside the horizon, including the ring singularity at r = 0 (see
section 3).

The thorn sets all of ADMBaseX's metric, curv, lapse, shift, dtlapse and
dtshift. Spatial and time derivatives of the metric are obtained by
forward-mode automatic differentiation (Arith::dual), so they are exact to
machine precision and need no ghost zones; every routine can therefore write
"everywhere".

Because the metric is stationary in these coordinates, dtlapse and dtshift are
identically zero. They are computed rather than hard-wired so that a boosted or
moving black hole would work without further changes.


2. Parameters

  mass              black hole mass M, must be positive
  spin              spin parameter a, WITH DIMENSION mass, so |spin| < mass
                    (this is a, not the dimensionless a/M, and not the angular
                    momentum J)
  r_regularization  how to regularize r near the ring singularity
  epsilon           regularization scale
  power             exponent used by the smooth regularization variants

The five ADMBaseX keywords initial_data, initial_lapse, initial_shift,
initial_dtlapse and initial_dtshift each accept the value "kerrschildx", and
each selects one independently scheduled routine. Selecting only some of them
is supported.


3. Regularizing the ring singularity

r is the Kerr auxiliary radius; the curvature singularity is at r = 0, which
lies on the grid. Three variants are available, following thorn Exact's
src/metrics/Kerr_KerrSchild.F:

  "max"        r <- sqrt(max(epsilon, r^2))
               The legacy behaviour, and still the default so that existing
               results are reproduced bit for bit. Note that here epsilon
               bounds r^2 rather than r. Continuous but NOT differentiable at
               r^2 = epsilon, which radiates grid-scale error in an evolution.

  "power-law"  r <- (r^power + epsilon^power)^(1/power)
               Smooth everywhere. power must be 2, 4 or 8, so that the root is
               a chain of square roots. Perturbs r at all radii, by
               ~epsilon^power / (power r^(power-1)).

  "parabolic"  For r < epsilon, r is replaced by an even polynomial in r that
               is matched smoothly at r = epsilon and stays positive at r = 0
               (a "stuffed" black hole). power must be 2, 4, 6 or 8. Exact
               outside r = epsilon.

For an evolution prefer "parabolic" with epsilon comfortably inside the
horizon, r_+ = M + sqrt(M^2 - a^2). See par/kerr-z4c-ah.par.


4. Scheduling

The routines run in ADMBaseX_InitialData and ADMBaseX_InitialGauge, so that an
evolution thorn reading ADMBaseX at CCTK_INITIAL (such as Z4c_Initial1) sees
the data. ADMBaseX only declares those groups when ODESolvers is active, so a
parameter file must activate ODESolvers even when it does not evolve.

To apply the analytic solution as a prescribed background at every time step
instead of once as initial data, additionally activate thorn Cowling, which
re-parents the initial-data groups into ADMBaseX_SetADMVars.


5. Parameter files

  par/kerrschild.par     minimal setup, dumps the data at iteration 0
  par/kerr-z4c-ah.par    Kerr with a/M = 0.8, Z4c, and AHFinderDirect locating
                         the horizon; documents the expected horizon diagnostics


6. References

Kerr-Schild form of the Kerr metric: see e.g. Misner, Thorne & Wheeler,
"Gravitation", Box 33.2, or the review in
<https://arxiv.org/abs/0706.0622> (Visser, "The Kerr spacetime: A brief
introduction").
